{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Be still and wait:</b> Some say it is from the root of תוחלת (hope). Others say it is from the root חיל as in יולדה (one giving birth), and this is the correct view. The word is doubled, like והתבונן." "<b>And the sense of 'to Him':</b> for His sake, like אחי הוא (my brother) and וסבול החיל (bear the burden) בגללו (on his account). Therefore it should be אל תתחר (do not be jealous) from חרון (wrath), and the bet in במצליח is בעבור (on account of). According to the interpretation 'compete with horses,' [it is] bear [it] and be still to HaShem; and in either case, with scoffers do not mingle."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :notes {1 "The argument presupposes reading דום והתחולל לה' as also governing the following phrases; the cryptic citations of אמרי לי אחי הוא and וסבול החיל appear to be fragmentary prooftexts for the paraphrastic sense of the lemmatic ל attached to לו/לה׳."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:29:56.834687044Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}